WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.823
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.823 --> 00:00:09.176
I think everyone has an origin story
that matters, and that's important.

00:00:09.176 --> 00:00:12.699
For me, my origin story began
at the age of seven years old.

00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:15.091
There's a lady in the hospital
fighting septicemia,

00:00:15.091 --> 00:00:17.538
which potentially could have
been a terminal illness.

00:00:17.538 --> 00:00:21.348
I discovered a comic book based on my
love for a fictitious character, Batman.

00:00:21.348 --> 00:00:24.999
And in that comic book there was
a character named Dr. Lucius Fox.

00:00:24.999 --> 00:00:28.249
He was a black man, he was an engineer,
he was a financier,

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:32.090
the CEO of Wayne Enterprises, and
he made all the gadgets for Batman.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.893
I read as much as I
could about Lucius Fox.

00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:37.168
He desperately clinging to all
the narratives that expose his brilliance

00:00:37.168 --> 00:00:40.864
in both engineering and making things, but
also strategy and building bridges and

00:00:40.864 --> 00:00:43.421
community with the chief of police and
the journalists.

00:00:43.421 --> 00:00:47.851
And so, he was the prototypical mentor
that I needed at that moment to see all of

00:00:47.851 --> 00:00:49.880
my gifts and talents in one person.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.192
Now, while he didn't
actually exist in real life,

00:00:53.192 --> 00:00:55.611
I gave myself a mission to become him.

00:00:55.611 --> 00:00:59.041
Growing up on the South side of Chicago,
it's quite difficult, I didn't fit in,

00:00:59.041 --> 00:01:01.248
I often was laughed at for
my dreams and aspirations.

00:01:01.248 --> 00:01:04.368
Because it's unheard of to say,
hey, I'm a nine year old child,

00:01:04.368 --> 00:01:06.342
I want to design gadgets for super heroes.

00:01:06.342 --> 00:01:09.273
People really believe that
you're just pretending, but

00:01:09.273 --> 00:01:11.560
I was serious about my mission and
my intent.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.852
As I matriculated through primary school
and to secondary school and into college,

00:01:15.852 --> 00:01:19.791
what I realized is that it didn't matter
what people thought about my dream.

00:01:19.791 --> 00:01:22.210
It only matter what I
thought about my dreams, so

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.163
I stopped seeking validation and
acceptance from others.

00:01:25.163 --> 00:01:29.449
I stopped seeking a yes and I started to
eliminate the way people can tell me no.

00:01:29.449 --> 00:01:32.517
Multiply that times my inquisitiveness and
my creativity, and

00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:35.603
I ended up being Nike's first
black design intern in 2001.

00:01:35.603 --> 00:01:38.532
I came from a traditional art and
design background.

00:01:38.532 --> 00:01:41.808
When I left Nike to come to Stanford,
I was a senior designer,

00:01:41.808 --> 00:01:45.535
I was the youngest person to reach
that title at the time when I held it.

00:01:45.535 --> 00:01:49.363
So getting to Stanford was just honestly
it seems like the natural outcome from

00:01:49.363 --> 00:01:50.005
my journey.

00:01:50.005 --> 00:01:53.332
But at the time as I was going
through looking at schools,

00:01:53.332 --> 00:01:58.046
looking at different environments, it
definitely was something that I felt was

00:01:58.046 --> 00:02:01.462
the highest aspiration for
me to strive for as a creative.

00:02:01.462 --> 00:02:04.821
Upon arrival at Stanford GSB,
I quite honestly was searching for

00:02:04.821 --> 00:02:07.998
a community of like minded
individuals who saw my talent is not

00:02:07.998 --> 00:02:11.621
being something that was integrated
vertically into a corporation.

00:02:11.621 --> 00:02:15.170
To something that can transform
the way we engage with society,

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:19.392
the way we have difficult conversations,
the way we design for belonging.

00:02:19.392 --> 00:02:23.529
Design is the filter you use to get to
the most ubiquitous outcome that helps

00:02:23.529 --> 00:02:25.047
the most amount of people.

00:02:25.047 --> 00:02:27.299
And so,
our job isn't to provide solutions,

00:02:27.299 --> 00:02:30.555
and most people think is drawing pictures,
producing products.

00:02:30.555 --> 00:02:34.963
It's really to ask the boldest
questions to move humanity forward.

00:02:34.963 --> 00:02:38.847
And so, I don't think we can move forward
anymore in this attention-based economy

00:02:38.847 --> 00:02:42.528
without taking design seriously, not just
letting us have a seat at the table.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:45.384
But letting us be the head of the table,
design the table,

00:02:45.384 --> 00:02:48.475
source the table because we have
skill sets that are valuable to

00:02:48.475 --> 00:02:52.698
really move humanity forward and move each
of these antiquated industries forward.

00:02:52.698 --> 00:02:56.810
We need to try new styles of leadership,
we need to radically shake up what it

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:00.406
means to be a CEO, what it means to
be a founder, the look and feel,

00:03:00.406 --> 00:03:02.295
what it means to be a board member.

00:03:02.295 --> 00:03:06.447
If you're at the GSB, if you're Black,
Latino, LGBTQ, woman, atheist, Muslim,

00:03:06.447 --> 00:03:08.816
Christian, whatever it
is that you identify us.

00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:12.274
You are welcome, and you are accepted,
and you are needed, and

00:03:12.274 --> 00:03:14.525
that is the thing you have to understand.

00:03:14.525 --> 00:03:17.959
There's a difference between being
wanted for your skill set and

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:20.463
needed to be part of
the texture of a community.

00:03:20.463 --> 00:03:23.816
We have to radically challenge the status
quo, and we have to do that with love,

00:03:23.816 --> 00:03:27.073
compassion, and the willingness to
sacrifice some of the opportunities for

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:28.598
the betterment of the collective.

00:03:28.598 --> 00:03:30.258
That is what it means to
be a servant leader and

00:03:30.258 --> 00:03:31.754
that is what we were taught at Stanford.

00:03:31.754 --> 00:03:39.281
[MUSIC]

00:03:39.281 --> 00:03:43.593
So I've often been asked,
did I become Lucius Fox?

00:03:43.593 --> 00:03:45.491
In my mind, I did, in my mind,

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:49.816
I've dedicated my life to using my
gifts and talents to serve others.

00:03:49.816 --> 00:03:52.592
I wake up daily and
I have a practice of asking myself,

00:03:52.592 --> 00:03:54.023
how can I kill my ego today?

00:03:54.023 --> 00:03:58.342
I put myself in situations where I serve
often, I reach out to people through text

00:03:58.342 --> 00:04:02.101
messages and voice message, and
ask them, how can I be there for you?

00:04:02.101 --> 00:04:04.810
And I think that's the embodiment
of the spirit of Lucius Fox,

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.149
he didn't have super powers.

00:04:06.149 --> 00:04:09.698
He was super compassionate, he was
intelligent, and he used that to help

00:04:09.698 --> 00:04:13.377
people who were out fighting the world's
greatest issues and challenges.

00:04:13.377 --> 00:04:16.305
So, simply put,
I believe I am becoming Lucius Fox, and

00:04:16.305 --> 00:04:19.782
it'll always be in motion because
there will always be problems for

00:04:19.782 --> 00:04:23.767
me to try to solve and they'll always be
challenges that can be met with love.

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:28.820
They'll always be people who feel left out
that I can advocate for and speak up for.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.930
So that's the mission of a person like
Lucius is to never feel satisfied

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:33.820
with the work,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.010
because the work isn't done until all
of us feel loved and feel the same.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.441
[MUSIC]
I'm Jason Mayden,

00:04:39.441 --> 00:04:42.641
I'm a designer,
I'm an entrepreneur from a GSB alum, but

00:04:42.641 --> 00:04:45.393
most importantly,
I am a father and I am a husband.

00:04:45.393 --> 00:04:55.393
[MUSIC]

